// A parenthesized non-callable followed by `(` is two operands with no
// operator between them: gcc and clang both reject `(x)(4)` ("called object
// is not a function or function pointer"). In an INITIALIZER the parse ends
// at the conditional-end short-circuit, which used to bind the pending `=` to
// the juxtaposed pair and return it: `int r = (x)(4)` built `int r = x = 4`
// (exit 0, r == 4). Every exit of parseExpression now finishes through
// Program::finish_expression, which refuses the leftover operand.
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int x = 3;
    int r = (x)(4);
    printf("%d\n", r);
    return 0;
}
